    "evidence_text": "They provided a dataset of tweets where the sentiments towards political candidates were labeled both by experts in political communication and by crowdworkers who were likely not domain experts.\n\naccurately for the dataset of political tweets provided by .\n\nFor sentiment analysis, we have a three-class problem (positive, negative, and neutral), where the classes are mutually exclusive.\n\nIn the sentiment analysis experiments, we found that a tweet may contain multiple sentiments.\n\nThe groundtruth labels contain 210 positive sentiments, 521 neutral sentiments, and 305 negative sentiments to the candidates.\n\nCrowdworkers correctly identified 62% of the neutral, 85% of the positive, and 92% of the negative sentiments.\n\nGoogle Cloud correctly identified 88% of the neutral sentiments, but only 3% of the positive, and 19% of the negative sentiments.\n\nof this work by the Hariri Institute for Computing and Computational Science & Engineering at Boston University (to L.G.) and a Google Faculty Research Award (to M.B. and L.G.) is gratefully acknowledged.\n\nAdditionally, we would like to thank Daniel Khashabi for his help in running the CogComp-NLP Python API and Mike Thelwal for his help with TensiStrength.",
